// A LITERAL LET IS SILENTLY IRREFUTABLE. `let 0 = x` refuses every value but
// zero, so it performs `abort()`, which nothing handles: the program must
// refuse with E_EffectUnhandled. It checks clean and runs, answering 7
// (measured 2026-09-25): the parser does not count a literal as a
// destructuring pattern, so the let binds nothing and tests nothing.
// `let Some(0) = x` refuses as it should.
//
// Retires when every non-variable pattern in let position desugars to the
// refutable arm it is.
fn f(x) = {
  let 0 = x
  7
}

fn main() = f(5)
